---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoFaceBlur from '@/components/tools/redact/vision/VideoFaceBlur.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="blur-faces-in-video" width="wide">
  <VideoFaceBlur client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to blur faces in a video</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop a video on the page. MP4, MOV, WebM and MKV files work if your browser can decode the video in them.</li>
      <li>Press Find faces. The tool checks 8 frames per second and follows each face through the video.</li>
      <li>Each person is listed with a picture. Click a person to keep their face visible. You can also click a face in the preview.</li>
      <li>Play the preview to check the result. Change the style or the strength if you want.</li>
      <li>Press Export video and download the file.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Turn on Find small faces for people far from the camera. The scan then takes about three times longer.
    </p>

    <h2>What runs on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The faces are found by a small face detection model (230 kB, MIT license) in a model runtime of 15 MB. Both
      download once from this site and are kept in your browser. A tracker gives each face a number and moves the blur
      smoothly between the checked frames. Your browser decodes and encodes the video with its own video codecs, in a
      background thread, so the page stays usable.
    </p>
    <p>
      On a recent laptop a 12-second 1080p clip takes about 7 seconds to scan and 2 seconds to export. Phones and older
      computers are slower. The progress bar shows the frame count and the time left, and you can cancel at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>The file you get</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>An MP4 file with H.264 video and the original sound. If your browser cannot encode H.264, you get a WebM file.</li>
      <li>Videos larger than 1080p are scaled down to 1920 by 1080 (or 1080 by 1920 for a vertical video).</li>
      <li>Location, device and other metadata from the original file are not copied.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to check that nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Open the developer tools of your browser (F12), select the Network tab and drop a video. You see the model files load
      from this site the first time and no request with your video. You can also load the tool once, turn off your network
      and use it.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Faces in profile, from behind, very small or in fast motion can be missed for a few frames. Watch the preview before you share the video.</li>
      <li>A face that appears between two checked frames can be visible for up to a tenth of a second.</li>
      <li>The browser must be able to encode video in a web page. Current versions of Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari can. Some browsers cannot decode HEVC or AV1 videos.</li>
      <li>The video is held in memory. Very long videos can fail on a phone; cut them into parts first.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/blur-face-in-photo">Blur Face in Photo</a> for photos,
      <a href="/face-blur-camera">Face Blur Camera</a> for your webcam, and
      <a href="/compress-video">Compress Video</a> to make the exported file smaller.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
